WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.150
So here it is, and we're going to talk a lot about

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:03.810
simulations.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.090
So here rather than rolling the die, I've

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:08.410
written a program to do it.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:14.630
We've already seen the code for rolling a die.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:20.370
And so to run the simulation, typically what we're

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:21.950
doing here is I'm giving you the goal.

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:25.330
For example, are we going to get five ones?

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:33.110
The number of trials, each trial in this case will be, say,

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:34.130
of length five.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:39.910
So I'm going to roll the same die 5 times, say, 1,000

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:40.730
different times.

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:44.170
And then just some text as to what I'm going to print.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:49.910
Almost all the simulations we look at are going to start

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:51.910
with lines that look a lot like that.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:57.050
We're going to initialize some variable, and then we're

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:59.690
going to run some number of trials.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:08.760
So in this case, we're going to get from the length of the

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.940
goal, so if the goal is five ones, then we're going to

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:13.220
roll the die five times.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:15.760
If it's 10 runs, we'll roll the die 10 times.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:23.480
So this is essentially one trial, one attempt, right?

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.160
And then we'll check the result.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:33.760
And if it has the property we want, in this case, it's

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.260
equal to the goal, then we're going to increment the total,

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:40.480
which we initialized up here, by one.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.500
So we'll keep track, which is just the counting.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:48.280
The number of trials that actually meet the goal.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.320
And then when we're done, what we're going to do is

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:57.560
divide the number that met the goal by the number of trials.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.380
Exactly the counting argument we just looked at, and then

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:03.380
we'll print the result.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:10.890
Almost every simulation we look at is going to have this

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:11.350
structure.

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:15.790
But be an outer loop, which is the number of trials, and

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:18.630
then inside, maybe we'll have a loop, or maybe it won't,

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:19.750
will be a single trial.

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:22.770
We'll sum up the results, and then we'll divide by the

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:23.490
number of trials.

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:25.390
All right, let's run it.

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:30.830
All right.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.820
So a couple of things are going to go on here.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:46.860
If you look at the code, as we've looked at it before,

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:50.600
what you're seeing is I'm computing the estimated

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:54.180
probability by the simulation, and I'm comparing it to the

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:57.460
actual probability, which we've already seen how to compute.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.130
So if you look at it, there are a couple of things to

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:02.550
look at.

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:08.230
The estimated probability is pretty close to the actual

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:10.830
probability, but not the same.

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:14.270
Let's go back to the PowerPoint.

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:20.580
Here are the results.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:26.320
And there are at least two question ways by this result.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:29.360
First of all, how did I know that this is what would get

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:29.740
printed?

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:32.200
Remember, this is random.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.460
How did I know that the estimate, well, there's

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:41.200
nothing random of the actual probability, but how did I

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.920
know that the estimated probability would be zero?

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:46.220
And why did it print it twice?

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:48.440
Because I messed up the PowerPoint, anyway.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:50.980
So how do I know what would get printed?

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:54.840
Well, a confession.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:02.240
Random.choice is not actually random.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:05.920
In fact, nothing we can do in a computer is

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:06.960
actually random.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:12.160
You can prove that it's impossible to build a computer

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:14.820
that actually generates truly random numbers.

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:20.680
What they do instead is generate numbers that are

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:21.840
called pseudorandom.

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:30.940
How do they do that?

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:36.560
They have an algorithm that, given one number, generates

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:38.240
the next number in a sequence.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:44.480
And they start that algorithm with a seed.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:52.160
Now, typically, they get that seed by reading the clock of

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:52.720
the computer.

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:57.280
So most computers have a clock that, say, keeps track of the

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:00.000
number of microseconds since January 1, 1978.
